/**
\page key_commands Key Commands

This page lists key commands supported by uvim. It mirrors `:help` and
includes common Normal/Insert/Visual/Command mode bindings.

\section keys_normal Normal mode

\subsection keys_normal_move Movement
- `h j k l` : left/down/up/right
- `j/k` and up/down arrows preserve the wanted column across lines, aligning
  to complete UTF-8 characters on shorter lines.
- `w b e` : next word / previous word / end of word
- `b` moves immediately; counts such as `2b` repeat the motion. The following
  key is processed normally. Use `:bd` to close a buffer.
- `0 ^ $` : start of line / first non-blank / end of line
- `gg` / `G` / `<n>G` : top / bottom / go to line n
- `Ctrl-f` / `Ctrl-b` : page down / page up
- `Ctrl-d` / `Ctrl-u` : half page down / up
- `zz` / `zt` / `zb` : center / top / bottom screen
- `%` : jump to matching bracket
- `Space-o` / `:functions` : browse current-buffer function signatures;
  type to filter, `Ctrl-j/k` to browse, `Enter` to jump, `Esc` to cancel
- `]f` / `[f` : next / previous function signature
- `]s` / `[s` : next / previous local variable declaration or call in the current function
- After either structural motion, `n` / `N` repeat forward / backward;
  `Esc`, a new search, leaving Normal mode, or changing buffers restores search repeats.
- Counts apply (`3]f`, `2n`); structural jumps do not wrap. `Ctrl-o` / `Ctrl-i`
  return through jump history. See the README's structural navigation section.

\subsection keys_normal_edit Editing
- `i I a A` : insert/append
- `o O` : new line below/above
- `x` / `X` : delete char at cursor / before cursor
- `s` / `S` : substitute char / line (enter insert)
- `C` / `D` : change / delete to end of line
- `J` : join lines
- `Ctrl-t` : reindent the enclosing multiline list, map, or argument list,
  including nested items, without a formatter; also available in Insert mode.
  Configure with `:set collectionindentkey=ctrl-y` or `off`, and
  `:set collectionindentwidth=0..16` (0 uses the buffer indent width).
- `r` / `R` : replace char / replace mode
- `~` : toggle case
- `p` / `P` : paste after / before
- `Y` : yank line
- `u` / `Ctrl-r` : undo / redo
- `.` : repeat last change

\subsection keys_normal_search Search
- `Ctrl-a` : project grep search
- `/` : project grep search
- `n` / `N` : next / previous match
- `*` / `#` : search word under cursor forward / backward
- `Space-n` : clear search highlights
- `:/...` / `:? ...` : regex search forward / backward (command mode)

\subsection keys_normal_marks Marks and jumps
- `m{a-z}` : set mark
- ``'{a-z}`` / `` `{a-z}`` : jump to mark
- `Ctrl-o` / `Ctrl-i` : jump back / forward
- `Ctrl-^` : alternate buffer
- `Ctrl-g` : show file info
- `Ctrl-l` : redraw

\subsection keys_normal_g g-commands
- `gd` : go to definition; in assembly buffers, opens cached x86/x64 or
  AArch64 instruction docs when built with `UVIM_ENABLE_ASM_DOCS`; run
  `uvim --asm-docs-fetch` to fetch Compiler Explorer/Godbolt asm docs into
  the cache on demand
- `gr` : find references
- `gf` : go to file under cursor
- `gh` : alternate file (header/source)
- `gb` : toggle git blame
- `gj` : show commit diff for current line when git blame is visible
- `gbv` : show commit diff for line under cursor
- `gs` : show C/C++ symbol size and member layout popup
- `gl` / `glf` : git log (repo / current file); `Ctrl-v` range selects

\subsection keys_normal_leader Leader (Space)
- `Space-e` : diagnostic popup on an LSP diagnostic row
- `Space-d` : go to definition
- `Space-g a` : show assembly instruction docs in a scrollable popup
- `Space-g d` : go to definition
- `Space-g r` : find references
- `Space-m e` : collect active-LSP errors and open a jump list
- `Space-m w` : collect active-LSP warnings and open a jump list
- `Space-y` / `Space-p` : yank/paste system clipboard
- `Space-q` : force quit
- `Space-x` : file browser
- `Space-e m` : emoji picker

\subsection keys_normal_splits Splits
- `:hs` : horizontal split
- `:vs` : vertical split
- in file browser, `:hs` : horizontal browser split
- in file browser, `:vs` : vertical browser split
- `Space-w c` : close the active editor pane
- `Ctrl-w` : browse open buffers; `Enter` opens the selected buffer only in
  the active pane
- `Ctrl-Shift-h/j/k/l` : focus pane left/down/up/right when built with
  `UVIM_ENABLE_MODERN_KEYBINDINGS` and `UVIM_ENABLE_MULTI_PANE_SPLITS`
- Disable the convenience split/buffer key layer in `uvim-config` by turning
  off `Modern keybindings`, or from CLI with
  `uvim-config --disable modern-keybindings`.
- Disable nested pane trees in `uvim-config` by turning off
  `Multi-pane splits`, or from CLI with
  `uvim-config --disable multi-pane-splits`. In that build, splits use the
  older single split pair and normal-mode `Ctrl-h` / `Ctrl-l` navigate buffers.
- Split panes refresh and draw their own LSP diagnostics by default. Disable
  that in `uvim-config` by turning off `Per-pane LSP`, or from CLI with
  `uvim-config --disable per-pane-lsp`.

\section keys_insert Insert mode
- Type text to insert
- `Enter` : continue indentation inside lists, maps, and argument lists;
  closing-only lines align with their opening line. Disable continuation with
  `:set continuationindent=false`.
- `Enter` inside `{}` / `[]` : open an indented blank line, keeping the closing
  delimiter aligned with the opening line. Configure `splitbraces` and
  `splitbrackets` independently with `:set <name>=true/false`.
- `[` : insert `[]`; `]` skips an existing closing bracket; Backspace inside
  an empty pair removes both brackets. Disable with `:set autobrackets=false`.
  Requires `autobraces`; in strings, also requires `autobracesinstrings`.
- `Ctrl-t` : reindent the enclosing multiline collection, staying in Insert mode;
  uses the same `collectionindentkey` and `collectionindentwidth` settings as
  Normal mode.
- `Esc` : return to normal mode
- Autocomplete appears as you type (if enabled)

\section keys_visual Visual mode
- `v` / `V` / `Ctrl-v` : character / line / block selection
- `y` : yank selection
- `d` : delete selection
- `c` : change selection

\section keys_command Command mode
- `:` : enter command mode
- `:bd` / `:bdelete` : close current buffer (bare `b` is word-backward movement)
- `Ctrl-k` / `Ctrl-j` : previous / next command in history
- `Ctrl-f` : fuzzy history search
- `:emitasm [flags]` : emit current C/C++ buffer assembly into a `.s` buffer
- `:asm [.|path] [--compiler clang|gcc|mlang|path] [--split] [--raw] [flags]` : compile and
  browse C/C++ or Mlang (`.mla`) assembly; `:asm . -O2` includes unsaved buffer edits. In the view,
  `j/k` browse numbered rows, `h/l` scroll horizontally, `i` toggles instruction
  documentation, `Ctrl-j/k` scrolls that popup, and `q`/`Esc` exits.
- `:asm . --split -O2` opens source and assembly side by side. `v` toggles
  the split. `Ctrl-Shift-h/l` (or `Alt-h/l` with `:set iskitty=false`) focuses
  the source/assembly pane. Navigation in either pane reveals matching rows
  in the other pane with a subtle green background and preserved syntax colors.
  Lines without emitted instructions and code from other files have no match.
- Select source lines with `V` or `v`, then run `:asm . [flags]` to show only
  assembly mapped to those lines. Compilation still uses the full buffer for
  includes/types/declarations. Character selections include whole touched lines.
  `--split` and the `--` flag picker work with selections; the source pane keeps
  original line numbers. Normal-mode `:asm .` shows the complete output again.
- The assembly browser requires the build option `UVIM_ENABLE_ASM_VIEW=ON`
  and auxiliary views. Only the `full` configurator preset enables it by default;
  toggle Assembly browser in `uvim-config` or use `--enable/--disable asm-view`.
- Instruction inspection prefers Compiler Explorer API documentation, with
  the inspected instruction at the top and operand forms/examples when provided.
  Built-in help stays visible while API docs load;
  only the loading indicator shows progress, after 300 ms of pending retrieval.
  Row changes restart a 300 ms debounce before refreshing documentation; the
  previous text stays visible while browsing. Fast responses cancel the indicator,
  and inspector requests do not produce queue/download status messages.
  `:set asmdocsapi=false` uses built-in help only.
- Assembly browsing starts each newly selected row at the beginning in both
  panes, including linked source jumps. `h/l` pans the current line; `0` returns
  it to the beginning. Pane switches and split toggles also reset horizontal pan.
- Assembly view titles include the target architecture, including cross targets
  such as `--target=aarch64-linux-gnu` or Mlang `--target-arch aarch64`.
- In the assembly browser, directly mapped code stays bright. Inferred labels,
  external-source rows, and unknown origins use progressively dimmer syntax
  colors; all unresolved rows are dimmed. Known external-source rows also have
  a dark red background (`theme.asm.external_bg`). Select external code to
  see its source file and line in the status bar when compiler debug information
  provides them. `:set asmdimunmapped=false` disables dimming.
- Typing `:asm . --` opens common compiler flags: up/down or `Ctrl-j/k`
  select, Enter/Tab inserts without compiling, and the next Enter compiles.
  `Esc` cancels the whole command.
- `.mla` files use `mlang -S -g`; optimization, raw/split views, source linking,
  and instruction inspection work as for C/C++. `:asm . --` suggests Mlang flags.
  Temporary snapshots beside the source preserve relative imports and require a
  writable source directory; compilation keeps the project indexing file intact.
- For C/C++, `:asm` automatically uses the matching `compile_commands.json` entry's compiler,
  flags, and working directory when found in source/ancestor or common build
  directories (or the configured clangd database). Prompt flags override inherited
  optimization, standards and macros; prompt include directories precede inherited
  paths of the same include kind.
  `--compiler` overrides the compiler. Prompt paths use the editor working
  directory; inherited paths use the database entry's directory. The status bar
  marks inherited settings with `compile_commands`.

\section keys_file_browser File browser
- `j/k` or arrows : move
- `Enter` : open file/directory
- `h` or `-` : parent directory
- `.` : toggle hidden files
- `i` : toggle gitignore
- `:` : command mode in browser
- `q` : close active browser pane; the final pane exits to editor/welcome
- `Q` : exit browser and save the current browser pane layout

\section keys_cli Startup flags
- `--no-git-index` : disable git-backed indexing for fuzzy find and grep
- `--no-gitignore` : disable `.gitignore` filtering for the session

\section keys_startup_notes Startup notes
- `uvim .` opens the file browser in the target directory
- with `editor.autodetectlsps: true`, auto-detected LSP startup is deferred
  until the first file is opened from the browser
- LSP initialization and C++ header-symbol indexing run in background tasks;
  continue editing while the status bar shows startup progress. Completion
  updates the status bar with ready/failure; `:tasks` lists background work.
- Document synchronization is queued in order, so a busy language server
  cannot block editor input while reading a newly opened file.

\section keys_grep Grep search mode
- Type to search
- `Enter` : open selected match
- `Ctrl-j` / `Ctrl-k` : move results
- `Ctrl-d` / `Ctrl-u` : half page down/up
- `Esc` : exit

\section keys_fuzzy Fuzzy file finder
- Type to search files
- `Enter` : open selected file
- `Ctrl-j` / `Ctrl-k` : move results
- `Ctrl-d` / `Ctrl-u` : half page down/up
- `Ctrl-n` : select/unselect current file for multi-open
- `Ctrl-o` : toggle filename-first ranking
- `Ctrl-i` : toggle `.gitignore` filtering
- `Esc` : exit

\section keys_buffer_browser Buffer browser
- `j/k` : navigate
- `Enter` : open
- `q` : quit

\section keys_misc Misc
- `Ctrl-e` then `Enter` : emoji picker
- `Space-e` then `m` : emoji picker
- Size popup (`gs` in C/C++): `j/k` scroll, `q` or `gs` close
- Diagnostics popup: `Ctrl-j/k` to navigate fixes, `Enter` to apply, `q` to close

*/
